WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.450
Everyone wants to live a long life, but no one wants to grow old.

2
00:00:04.450 --> 00:00:11.460
So is old age really nothing more than a slow letting go, or are there things we can only see once our hair turns grey?

3
00:00:11.460 --> 00:00:15.750
Two thousand years ago, Cicero gave one answer to that question.

4
00:00:15.750 --> 00:00:19.720
Nine hundred years ago, Yusuf Has Hajib gave another.

5
00:00:19.720 --> 00:00:24.330
And today, each of us who looks in the mirror gives an answer of our own.

6
00:00:32.530 --> 00:00:37.300
It's morning, and you're leaning toward the mirror in the pale bathroom light.

7
00:00:37.300 --> 00:00:41.270
Toothbrush in hand, your mind already on the day ahead.

8
00:00:41.270 --> 00:00:47.800
Then you see it: at your temple, standing out from all the other strands, a single white hair.

9
00:00:47.800 --> 00:00:50.010
You pause for a moment.

10
00:00:50.010 --> 00:00:53.180
Pull it out, or pretend it isn't there?

11
00:00:53.180 --> 00:00:56.190
It isn't really the hair that stops you.

12
00:00:56.190 --> 00:00:59.200
It's everything it heralds.

13
00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:06.690
The lines at the corners of your eyes, the breath that quickens at the top of the stairs, the old friend whose name you just can't recall.

14
00:01:06.690 --> 00:01:11.140
That one white hair holds a quiet question: What am I losing?

15
00:01:11.140 --> 00:01:17.030
And right behind it comes a question we ask far less often: What is coming to me along the way?

16
00:01:17.030 --> 00:01:21.160
Humanity has been asking this question for thousands of years.

17
00:01:21.160 --> 00:01:26.410
But today it takes a sharper form than ever before: Is old age a disease?

18
00:01:26.410 --> 00:01:31.020
This is no longer just a question for philosophers.

19
00:01:31.020 --> 00:01:37.390
It is being debated in laboratories, in investor meetings, and around the tables where health policy is made.

20
00:01:37.390 --> 00:01:47.280
To some biologists, aging is simply the sum of damage piling up in our cells, of shortening chromosome tips, of repair mechanisms breaking down.

21
00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:53.170
In other words, not nature's inevitable calendar, but an engineering problem waiting to be solved.

22
00:01:53.170 --> 00:01:59.220
Those who hold this view argue that if we don't call aging a disease, we will never even try to treat it.

23
00:01:59.220 --> 00:02:05.750
The longevity companies drawing billions of dollars in investment from Silicon Valley are built on exactly this idea.

24
00:02:05.750 --> 00:02:10.200
For a time, the debate even found its way into official documents.

25
00:02:10.200 --> 00:02:19.290
While preparing the eleventh revision of the International Classification of Diseases, the World Health Organization considered placing the term "old age" in one of its codes.

26
00:02:19.290 --> 00:02:22.140
The objections were fierce.

27
00:02:22.140 --> 00:02:30.110
Many physicians and researchers on aging warned that this amounted to treating older people as sick simply for being old, and that real,

28
00:02:30.110 --> 00:02:38.720
treatable illnesses would end up being brushed aside as just "old age." In the end the wording was changed, replaced by a far more careful definition,

29
00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:47.170
something like "ageing-associated decline in intrinsic capacity." This battle over the placement of a single phrase reveals the heart of the matter.

30
00:02:47.170 --> 00:02:52.580
The name we give to old age shapes how we see the people who are living it.

31
00:02:52.580 --> 00:02:55.270
And this is where the tension comes from.

32
00:02:55.270 --> 00:03:02.600
If old age is a disease, then the old person is a patient, and an entire life becomes a chart of losses.

33
00:03:02.600 --> 00:03:07.530
But if old age is not a disease, what do we make of the losses people truly suffer?

34
00:03:07.530 --> 00:03:15.180
Isn't dressing up aching knees, a fading memory, and friends departing one by one as "maturity" its own kind of denial?

35
00:03:15.180 --> 00:03:18.670
Here, science draws an interesting distinction.

36
00:03:18.670 --> 00:03:23.600
The psychologist Raymond Cattell proposed thinking of intelligence in two layers.

37
00:03:23.600 --> 00:03:33.170
The first he called fluid intelligence: solving a new problem quickly, spotting an unfamiliar pattern, holding several things in mind at once.

38
00:03:33.170 --> 00:03:37.780
This ability peaks in young adulthood and then slowly declines.

39
00:03:37.780 --> 00:03:45.910
The second he called crystallized intelligence: accumulated knowledge, vocabulary, the skill of recognizing and weighing situations.

40
00:03:45.910 --> 00:03:50.680
This layer holds steady well into later life, and often it even grows.

41
00:03:50.680 --> 00:03:55.610
A twenty-five-year-old programmer can master a new programming language in a week.

42
00:03:55.610 --> 00:04:03.900
A sixty-five-year-old judge, meanwhile, can read an entire case from the hesitation in a single sentence spoken by a witness.

43
00:04:03.900 --> 00:04:08.030
Both are intelligence, but of entirely different kinds.

44
00:04:08.030 --> 00:04:13.120
So when we ask, "Are we losing something?", we are asking the wrong question.

45
00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:15.650
Of course we are losing something.

46
00:04:15.650 --> 00:04:23.300
The real question is this: Does something arrive to take the place of what we lose, and does what arrives make up for what has gone?

47
00:04:23.300 --> 00:04:32.870
One of the first thinkers to ask this question systematically was an aging Roman who, two thousand years ago, took up his pen as his country stood on the brink of civil war.

48
00:04:32.870 --> 00:04:36.680
The year is forty-four, before the birth of Christ.

49
00:04:36.680 --> 00:04:40.010
The Roman Republic is in its death throes.

50
00:04:40.010 --> 00:04:43.500
Marcus Tullius Cicero is sixty-two years old.

51
00:04:43.500 --> 00:04:52.110
He has recently lost his beloved daughter Tullia, he has been all but pushed out of politics, and he spends his days at his country houses outside the city.

52
00:04:52.110 --> 00:04:59.280
And it is in these dark days that he writes a short, almost cheerful little book: Cato Maior de Senectute, or On Old Age.

53
00:04:59.280 --> 00:05:03.090
Cicero does not speak in his own voice.

54
00:05:03.090 --> 00:05:10.740
He hands the floor to Cato the Elder, an elderly statesman who had lived a century earlier and was famous for his sternness and his honesty.

55
00:05:10.740 --> 00:05:13.590
In the book, Cato is eighty-four.

56
00:05:13.590 --> 00:05:18.040
Two young friends ask him how he manages to carry old age so lightly.

57
00:05:18.040 --> 00:05:24.250
Cato gathers the reasons people find old age miserable under four headings, and weighs each one in turn.

58
00:05:24.250 --> 00:05:29.500
The first is that old age pulls a person away from work, away from an active life.

59
00:05:29.500 --> 00:05:32.510
Cato answers with the image of a ship.

60
00:05:32.510 --> 00:05:38.400
On board, some sailors climb the masts, some run about the deck, some bail out the bilge.

61
00:05:38.400 --> 00:05:43.810
The helmsman, though, sits quietly at the stern and simply holds the tiller.

62
00:05:43.810 --> 00:05:49.060
No one calls him lazy, because his work is less noisy, but more important.

63
00:05:49.060 --> 00:05:57.830
Great deeds, Cato says, are not done with muscle, speed, or agility, but with wisdom, with standing, with sound judgment.

64
00:05:57.830 --> 00:06:00.680
And these do not diminish with age.

65
00:06:00.680 --> 00:06:02.570
They grow.

66
00:06:02.570 --> 00:06:06.540
Second, old age weakens the body.

67
00:06:06.540 --> 00:06:09.550
Cato does not deny this.

68
00:06:09.550 --> 00:06:16.240
But he suggests that just as we want the strength of a bull in youth, in old age we should want what befits old age.

69
00:06:16.240 --> 00:06:22.610
Every age has a strength of its own; rather than mourning what is gone, we should make use of what remains.

70
00:06:22.610 --> 00:06:29.460
He tells us that he himself learned Greek in his old age, seizing on the language as if quenching a long thirst.

71
00:06:29.460 --> 00:06:33.110
Third, old age deprives us of pleasure.

72
00:06:33.110 --> 00:06:38.680
Here Cato's answer takes on its sharpest form: this is not a loss, but a gift.

73
00:06:38.680 --> 00:06:45.210
The passions of the body cloud the judgment of youth, driving us toward greed, anger and regret.

74
00:06:45.210 --> 00:06:54.140
Old age turns down that noise, and leaves behind the quiet pleasures of conversation, of reading, of working the soil, of tending vineyards and gardens.

75
00:06:54.140 --> 00:07:01.790
It is no accident that one of Cato's favorite pleasures is farming; the seed falls to the earth, grows, and bears fruit.

76
00:07:01.790 --> 00:07:05.600
It is the joy of someone who knows how to wait patiently.

77
00:07:05.600 --> 00:07:09.570
The fourth, and the heaviest, is the nearness of death.

78
00:07:09.570 --> 00:07:13.540
And here we find what may be Cato's most beautiful image.

79
00:07:13.540 --> 00:07:23.270
The death of the young, he says, is like a flame put out by a flood of water; the death of the old is like a fire that goes out on its own once its fuel is spent.

80
00:07:23.270 --> 00:07:29.000
To pick unripe fruit from the tree, you have to force it; ripe fruit falls of its own accord.

81
00:07:29.000 --> 00:07:35.370
Cato does not romanticize death; but he invites us to see it as the natural completion of a life.

82
00:07:35.370 --> 00:07:39.660
Read today, Cicero's defense may sound a little too optimistic.

83
00:07:39.660 --> 00:07:45.070
Not everyone has vineyards and gardens, or a distinguished career in the Senate behind them.

84
00:07:45.070 --> 00:07:52.400
Cicero knows this too, and he gives Cato an important line: the greatest protection in old age is a good life built in youth.

85
00:07:52.400 --> 00:08:00.210
In other words, old age is not something that suddenly befalls us; it is the top floor of a structure we have spent years building.

86
00:08:00.210 --> 00:08:06.420
Now let us travel some five hundred years back from Rome, and thousands of kilometers to the east.

87
00:08:06.420 --> 00:08:09.270
The state of Lu, in China.

88
00:08:09.270 --> 00:08:17.880
In the Lunyu, the book known as the Analects, compiled by his students after his death, Confucius sums up his own life in a single paragraph.

89
00:08:17.880 --> 00:08:22.810
That paragraph may well be the shortest autobiography in human history.

90
00:08:22.810 --> 00:08:27.100
At fifteen, he says, I set my heart on learning.

91
00:08:27.100 --> 00:08:30.590
At thirty, I stood on my own feet.

92
00:08:30.590 --> 00:08:33.920
At forty, I was freed from doubt.

93
00:08:33.920 --> 00:08:38.530
At fifty, I understood the path that Heaven had laid out for me.

94
00:08:38.530 --> 00:08:44.260
At sixty, my ear learned to sift what it heard; nothing I heard could shake me any longer.

95
00:08:44.260 --> 00:08:49.990
And at seventy, I could follow the desires of my heart, without ever straying from what is right.

96
00:08:49.990 --> 00:08:53.640
That last sentence deserves a moment's pause.

97
00:08:53.640 --> 00:09:00.010
Confucius was a thinker who spent his whole life urging people to follow rules, rituals and moral discipline.

98
00:09:00.010 --> 00:09:07.340
Yet the final stop he reaches is not a list of rules: it is a state in which desire and virtue are no longer separate things.

99
00:09:07.340 --> 00:09:13.070
The young person forces themselves to do what is right, suppressing what rises from within.

100
00:09:13.070 --> 00:09:22.320
For the sage of seventy, that struggle is over; whatever he wants is right, because at the end of a long cultivation, his desires themselves have been transformed.

101
00:09:22.320 --> 00:09:28.530
When we set Cicero and Confucius side by side, we notice an interesting difference.

102
00:09:28.530 --> 00:09:36.660
Cicero defends old age; he shields it against its accusers, weighing each loss one by one and showing how it is made good.

103
00:09:36.660 --> 00:09:40.630
Confucius does not even feel the need for a defense.

104
00:09:40.630 --> 00:09:46.520
In his telling, life is already a staircase climbing upward, each step higher than the last.

105
00:09:46.520 --> 00:09:48.890
Loss never even comes up.

106
00:09:48.890 --> 00:09:52.700
Modern psychology lends surprising support to this optimism.

107
00:09:52.700 --> 00:10:03.870
Research by the Stanford University psychologist Laura Carstensen has shown that older people, on average, experience fewer negative emotions than the young, and regulate their emotions better.

108
00:10:03.870 --> 00:10:13.920
Carstensen explains this through our perception of time: when a long life stretches out ahead of us, we turn toward meeting new people, gathering knowledge, investing in the future.

109
00:10:13.920 --> 00:10:22.530
But when we sense that our time is limited, our priorities shift; we turn toward deep relationships, meaningful moments, the present.

110
00:10:22.530 --> 00:10:29.860
Researchers call this "the paradox of aging": in the very years when the body declines, the heart often grows lighter.

111
00:10:29.860 --> 00:10:33.350
But the paradox has another side.

112
00:10:33.350 --> 00:10:37.000
Confucius's staircase does not lead upward for everyone.

113
00:10:37.000 --> 00:10:44.330
Sometimes, at the very place we believe we have risen to, we look back, and only then do we feel the weight of what we have left behind.

114
00:10:44.330 --> 00:10:52.940
That feeling, the weight of white hair and the longing for youth, is voiced in all its nakedness by one of the first great philosophical works written in Turkic.

115
00:10:52.940 --> 00:11:01.070
Toward the end of the year ten sixty-nine, in the lands of the Karakhanids, a man from Balasagun completes a work that has taken him eighteen months.

116
00:11:01.070 --> 00:11:03.760
His name is Yusuf.

117
00:11:03.760 --> 00:11:13.330
He presents his book to Tabghach Bughra Khan in Kashgar, and the khan rewards him by making him an official of the court; that is why history remembers him as Khass Hajib.

118
00:11:13.330 --> 00:11:20.020
The book is called Kutadgu Bilig, the knowledge that brings good fortune, the wisdom that gives happiness.

119
00:11:20.020 --> 00:11:25.110
It is a book about the state, about justice, and about the good life.

120
00:11:25.110 --> 00:11:31.160
But within it there are also lines written not in the voice of a courtier, but in the voice of a man growing old.

121
00:11:31.160 --> 00:11:38.810
Yusuf, early in the book, while explaining why he wrote his work, suddenly turns to his own life and laments his lost youth.

122
00:11:38.810 --> 00:11:44.060
He says that youth came and went like a guest, that he searched for it but could not find it.

123
00:11:44.060 --> 00:11:53.630
He writes that his raven-black hair has turned white as a swan's feather, that his once upright body has bent, that what was taut as a bowstring has curved like the bow itself.

124
00:11:53.630 --> 00:11:56.480
These images are not mere ornament.

125
00:11:56.480 --> 00:12:06.370
In the eleventh century, a Turkish poet experiences what we feel when we look in the mirror one morning: a change as jarring as darkened hair turning into a white swan.

126
00:12:06.370 --> 00:12:10.340
But Kutadgu Bilig does not remain inside this sorrow.

127
00:12:10.340 --> 00:12:16.230
When we look at the work as a whole, we see that Yusuf also regards old age through another lens.

128
00:12:16.230 --> 00:12:27.560
The book is a dialogue built around four central characters: the ruler Kuntoghdi represents justice, the vizier Aytoldi represents fortune, the vizier's son Ogdulmish represents intellect,

129
00:12:27.560 --> 00:12:34.410
and the reclusive dervish Odgurmish represents the end, that is, the close of life and what lies beyond it.

130
00:12:34.410 --> 00:12:39.020
Odgurmish is the character who reminds us of the transience of the world.

131
00:12:39.020 --> 00:12:47.630
When he is summoned to the palace, he does not wish to go; for him, life is short, the world is an inn, and a person is merely a traveler.

132
00:12:47.630 --> 00:12:52.080
In Yusuf's world, old age is the age of exactly this awareness.

133
00:12:52.080 --> 00:12:56.690
The young man believes the world is permanent, believes himself immortal.

134
00:12:56.690 --> 00:13:00.340
The white beard is where that illusion comes to an end.

135
00:13:00.340 --> 00:13:09.270
In many verses of Kutadgu Bilig, we hear of the value of knowledge and experience, and of the need to seek counsel from those who have seen much of life.

136
00:13:09.270 --> 00:13:18.040
Beside a ruler there should be not only young and ambitious men, but also people who carry the years on their backs, who know when to stay silent and when to speak.

137
00:13:18.040 --> 00:13:21.850
Words gain weight only when a lifetime stands behind them.

138
00:13:21.850 --> 00:13:25.980
This idea lives on in other forms deep within Turkic culture.

139
00:13:25.980 --> 00:13:38.750
In villages and nomadic camps from Central Asia to Anatolia, the word "aksakal," meaning "white beard," describes not simply a man with a white beard, but the elder of the community, whose word is respected,

140
00:13:38.750 --> 00:13:43.520
who settles disputes, and who is consulted on decisions.

141
00:13:43.520 --> 00:13:50.530
In Kazakhstan and Kyrgyzstan, even today, there are councils of aksakals at the neighborhood and village level.

142
00:13:50.530 --> 00:13:53.700
Here, age is not a loss but a rank.

143
00:13:53.700 --> 00:14:02.790
About a century after Yusuf, a legend told of Khoja Ahmad Yasawi, who lived in Turkestan, reveals the bond between old age and meaning even more sharply.

144
00:14:02.790 --> 00:14:13.160
According to the legend, when Yasawi reached sixty-three, the age at which the Prophet Muhammad died, he considered it disrespectful to walk the earth any longer than the Prophet had,

145
00:14:13.160 --> 00:14:19.690
and he withdrew into an underground cell he had dug for himself, spending the rest of his life there.

146
00:14:19.690 --> 00:14:24.780
This should be read not as a historical document, but as a hagiographic tale.

147
00:14:24.780 --> 00:14:28.910
Yet such tales, too, tell us what a community values.

148
00:14:28.910 --> 00:14:36.240
Here, old age is seen as the time to withdraw from the world, to know one's measure, to surrender to something greater than oneself.

149
00:14:36.240 --> 00:14:40.690
There is a tension between the lament in Kutadgu Bilig and this legend.

150
00:14:40.690 --> 00:14:46.100
One grieves for youth slipping away; the other willingly turns away from the world.

151
00:14:46.100 --> 00:14:52.310
But both know the same thing: old age is where a person comes face to face with the time they have lived.

152
00:14:52.310 --> 00:14:57.400
In youth, time is an abstract notion; it seems as if it will never run out.

153
00:14:57.400 --> 00:15:03.450
With white hair, time becomes concrete, measurable, something that can be felt.

154
00:15:03.450 --> 00:15:07.900
That is why Yusuf's swan-white hair is both a mourning and an awakening.

155
00:15:07.900 --> 00:15:12.510
But what if the community no longer recognizes the value of this awakening?

156
00:15:12.510 --> 00:15:16.160
What if no one is left to listen to the aksakal's words?

157
00:15:16.160 --> 00:15:20.130
It is true that in the old world, the white beard was a rank.

158
00:15:20.130 --> 00:15:26.180
But in the modern world, that same white beard is increasingly becoming a cloak of invisibility.

159
00:15:26.180 --> 00:15:32.870
In nineteen seventy, in Paris, sixty-two-year-old Simone de Beauvoir publishes a thick volume.

160
00:15:32.870 --> 00:15:36.200
Its title is La Vieillesse, that is, Old Age.

161
00:15:36.200 --> 00:15:44.170
Most of us know Beauvoir from the book she wrote twenty years earlier, describing how women are pushed into the position of the "other" in society.

162
00:15:44.170 --> 00:15:48.460
In Old Age, she turns that same sharp gaze on a different group.

163
00:15:48.460 --> 00:15:56.430
In her view, modern society turns the elderly into an "other" as well; and it does so so quietly that no one even notices.

164
00:15:56.430 --> 00:16:02.320
One of Beauvoir's most striking observations is that old age is not something felt from within.

165
00:16:02.320 --> 00:16:08.210
A person does not feel old, she says; the self inside always remains the same.

166
00:16:08.210 --> 00:16:11.060
It is others who tell us that we are old.

167
00:16:11.060 --> 00:16:19.350
We realize it when someone offers us a seat on the bus, when a young person speaks to us slowly and loudly, when no one asks for our opinion.

168
00:16:19.350 --> 00:16:24.760
Old age is first born in the gaze of others, and then we turn that gaze upon ourselves.

169
00:16:24.760 --> 00:16:28.250
Beauvoir's analysis goes further still.

170
00:16:28.250 --> 00:16:33.660
In a capitalist society, she says, a person's worth is measured by their productivity.

171
00:16:33.660 --> 00:16:38.910
Someone who has left their working years behind becomes, by this reckoning, a burden.

172
00:16:38.910 --> 00:16:44.640
Retirement is presented as a reward, but more often than not it means being set aside.

173
00:16:44.640 --> 00:16:51.170
That is why, for Beauvoir, the misery of old age is a social problem before it is a biological one.

174
00:16:51.170 --> 00:16:57.860
How we treat the old is, in fact, a mirror of how we treat human beings throughout their entire lives.

175
00:16:57.860 --> 00:17:05.830
Robert Butler, an American psychiatrist, had given this attitude a name one year before Beauvoir's book appeared: ageism.

176
00:17:05.830 --> 00:17:12.840
Like racism and sexism, it means judging people solely by their age and forcing them into stereotypes.

177
00:17:12.840 --> 00:17:25.450
When we read the phrase "young and dynamic" in a job listing, when we assume an older person won't understand technology, when we brush off an elder's opinion as "old-fashioned," we are inside that stereotype.

178
00:17:25.450 --> 00:17:30.700
This brings us to the nursing home, because that is where this debate becomes most concrete.

179
00:17:30.700 --> 00:17:36.590
In Türkiye, the share of the population over sixty-five has passed ten percent in recent years.

180
00:17:36.590 --> 00:17:44.720
That is a profound transformation for a society that has traditionally cared for its elders at home, where three generations lived under one roof.

181
00:17:44.720 --> 00:17:53.170
Urbanization, shrinking households, women in the workforce, migration all strain the old form of care within the family.

182
00:17:53.170 --> 00:17:57.460
Nursing homes, care homes and day centers are filling that gap.

183
00:17:57.460 --> 00:18:01.910
It would be unfair to see the nursing home simply as a symbol of abandonment.

184
00:18:01.910 --> 00:18:08.920
For many older people, it means escape from loneliness, access to health care, and a community of their peers.

185
00:18:08.920 --> 00:18:13.370
For many families, it is the only way to provide proper care.

186
00:18:13.370 --> 00:18:17.020
The real question is not the building itself.

187
00:18:17.020 --> 00:18:21.790
It is whether, inside that building, a person is still seen as a subject.

188
00:18:21.790 --> 00:18:27.520
Do they decide for themselves what to eat in the morning, what to do through the day, whom to see?

189
00:18:27.520 --> 00:18:30.050
Are they listened to?

190
00:18:30.050 --> 00:18:34.180
Or are they merely a body to be looked after, fed and given medicine?

191
00:18:34.180 --> 00:18:37.990
Beauvoir's answer to this is both simple and difficult.

192
00:18:37.990 --> 00:18:51.080
The only way to keep old age from becoming a pitiful parody of our former life, she says, is to go on pursuing ends that give our lives meaning: devotion to others, to a community, to a cause; intellectual,

193
00:18:51.080 --> 00:18:54.250
social or creative work.

194
00:18:54.250 --> 00:18:59.980
In other words, what will save old age is not clinging to the past but still having a future.

195
00:18:59.980 --> 00:19:04.270
This sentence casts our entire journey in a new light.

196
00:19:04.270 --> 00:19:08.240
Cicero's Cato was learning Greek at eighty-four.

197
00:19:08.240 --> 00:19:14.450
Confucius described what he had reached at seventy not as a stopping point but as a state of being.

198
00:19:14.450 --> 00:19:20.340
Even as Yusuf Khass Hajib lamented his lost youth, he still had a work ahead of him to finish.

199
00:19:20.340 --> 00:19:27.030
What they all share is a view of old age not as an ending, but as a time still being lived.

200
00:19:27.030 --> 00:19:34.200
When Erik Erikson divided human development into stages, he called the final one the tension between integrity and despair.

201
00:19:34.200 --> 00:19:43.770
A person nearing the end of life looks back and either accepts what they have lived as a whole, or is crushed under the weight of missed opportunities and mistakes they could not set right.

202
00:19:43.770 --> 00:19:51.900
For Erikson, the person who reaches integrity is one who can embrace their life as it was, and find a kind of peace even in the face of death.

203
00:19:51.900 --> 00:19:55.070
The name of that peace is wisdom.

204
00:19:55.070 --> 00:19:58.880
But wisdom is not only an individual achievement.

205
00:19:58.880 --> 00:20:05.250
Whether a person grows wise or despairing in old age depends partly on how the people around them see them.

206
00:20:05.250 --> 00:20:09.220
The aksakal's word carries weight only if someone is listening.

207
00:20:09.220 --> 00:20:13.990
Cato's steering earns respect only if those on board know how much the rudder matters.

208
00:20:13.990 --> 00:20:23.080
When a society makes its elders invisible, it impoverishes not only those people but its own future, because sooner or later everyone will reach that age.

209
00:20:23.080 --> 00:20:28.810
Perhaps the question we began with, "losing or gaining?", was framed a little incompletely.

210
00:20:28.810 --> 00:20:36.140
Much of what we lose as we age is beyond our control: speed, strength, sometimes health, the people we love.

211
00:20:36.140 --> 00:20:44.430
What we gain, though, depends largely on two things: how we have lived over the years, and whether the people around us still make room for us.

212
00:20:44.430 --> 00:20:46.800
The first is our responsibility.

213
00:20:46.800 --> 00:20:49.490
The second belongs to all of us.

214
00:20:49.490 --> 00:20:54.100
And so the white hair you saw in the mirror that morning takes on a different meaning.

215
00:20:54.100 --> 00:20:57.270
Yes, it heralds a loss.

216
00:20:57.270 --> 00:21:07.160
But it is also an invitation: to build the years ahead starting now, as Cicero said; to hold on to a future, as Beauvoir said; and perhaps most important of all,

217
00:21:07.160 --> 00:21:12.570
to look at the older person beside us today with the same eyes we hope will one day look at us.

218
00:21:12.570 --> 00:21:16.540
We've come to the end of today's episode of Nöron Felsefe.

219
00:21:16.540 --> 00:21:19.390
Until we meet again with a new story.
